        <div id="decibels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What the Decibel Numbers Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Decibels are logarithmic, which makes small numeric differences bigger than they look. A rise of about 10 decibels is generally perceived as roughly twice as loud, so a unit rated at 43 dBA on low is noticeably quieter than one rated at 52, not slightly quieter. For context, the World Health Organization's community noise guidelines suggest continuous background noise in bedrooms should stay around 30 dBA for good sleep. No compressor dehumidifier we found claims to meet that at a meter away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maker figures are also not standardized the way capacity ratings are. One maker may measure at one meter in a quiet chamber, another at a different distance, and many do not say. Some quote only the high-speed figure, some only the low. Danby's two published sheets for the same 22-pint Wi-Fi model even disagree, listing 48 and 50 dB on one and 56 dB on the other. Treat published figures as a way to compare models from the same maker and a rough guide across makers, not as precise predictions for your room.
          </p>

          <p>
            The type of noise matters as much as the level. A compressor dehumidifier makes two sounds: a steady fan whoosh and the lower hum of the compressor, which starts and stops as the humidistat cycles. Many light sleepers find the cycling more disruptive than the steady sound. Thermoelectric units like the Pro Breeze PB-03 have no compressor, so they produce only fan noise; that is why they are quieter, and also why they remove so little water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distance and surfaces change everything. A unit three feet from your head on a hard floor will sound louder than its rating; the same unit across the room on a rug, facing away from the bed, will sound quieter. Bedrooms with hard floors and bare walls reflect sound. If you are sensitive to noise, place the unit as far from the bed as the cord allows and point its exhaust away from you.
          </p>

