        <div id="sleep-features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sleep Modes, Lights and Alarms
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sleep mode on a dehumidifier usually means two things: the fan drops to its lowest speed and stays there, and on some models the display dims. Sylvane describes the Honeywell TP30AWKN's sleep mode as running on a low fan speed for quieter dehumidification. That is useful, but it also means slower drying, so a sleep mode works best in a room that has already been brought down to target during the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Display light is the complaint we see most often from bedroom buyers. Many dehumidifiers have bright LED digits and indicator lights that stay on all night. If a model does not offer a display-off or dim setting, a small piece of opaque tape over the brightest indicators is a cheap fix; just do not cover the bucket-full or error lights entirely, because you need to see those in the morning. Smart models with an app, like the Danby and Honeywell picks, let you check status from your phone so the panel matters less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bucket alarms matter more than people expect. A full bucket stops the compressor, which is safe, but some models also beep. Retailers describe the GE ADYR22LC as sounding a 10-second alarm when the bucket is full or missing, which is short but will wake a light sleeper. The fix is to empty the bucket at bedtime so it has its full capacity overnight, or to use an app alert instead of an audible one on a smart model.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timers and schedules are underrated. Running the dehumidifier hard in the afternoon and early evening, with the door closed, then switching to low or off at bedtime gets the room dry before you sleep and keeps the night quiet. The GE has Smart Dry, which GE says adjusts the fan speed automatically based on room humidity; the Danby has a 24-hour timer and app control. Any of these lets you set it and forget it rather than relying on willpower at midnight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sizing and the 2019 DOE Change
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dehumidifier capacity is measured in pints of water removed per day, and the number changed in 2019. That year the Department of Energy moved the standard test from 80 degrees Fahrenheit and 60 percent humidity to 65 degrees and 60 percent. Cooler air holds less water, so the same machine removes less in the test and earns a smaller number. A unit once sold as 30 pints is now typically rated around 20 to 22 pints, and Honeywell labels its TP30 line exactly that way: 30 pints, 20 pints under the 2019 DOE standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a bedroom, that means a 20 to 22 pint unit under the current test is the right size for almost any room. It is enough for a large primary bedroom or a damp basement bedroom, and it is the smallest compressor class most major brands still make. Bigger units, 35 or 50 pints, are designed for basements and are larger, heavier and usually louder at their lowest speed than a bedroom needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thermoelectric units are rated differently, and generously. Pro Breeze rates the PB-03 at about 18 ounces a day, roughly a pint, at 86 degrees and 80 percent humidity, conditions far kinder than the DOE test. In a 70-degree bedroom at 60 percent humidity, it will remove less. That is fine for a small guest room or a closet, and not enough for a bedroom with a real moisture problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage claims in square feet are marketing. A 22-pint unit rated for 1,500 square feet will comfortably handle a bedroom of 150 to 300 square feet, and that is the point: in a bedroom you want a unit that reaches the target quickly and then idles, not one that runs flat out all night. Our <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier sizing guide</a> covers the math for larger or damper spaces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat-placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Heat and Placement
          </h2>

          <p>
            A compressor dehumidifier warms the room it is in. It pulls heat out of the air at the cold coil to condense water, then dumps that heat, plus the heat from its own motor and compressor, back into the room at the warm coil. Nearly all the electricity it uses ends up as heat, and condensing water releases more. In a basement that can be welcome; in a summer bedroom it can make the room feel stuffy, which is the opposite of what most sleepers want.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical answer is timing and placement. Run the dehumidifier during the day and early evening, with the door closed, and let the air conditioner or an open window handle the temperature at night. If you must run it overnight in summer, put it on the far side of the room from the bed and point its exhaust away from you. Thermoelectric units add less heat because they draw little power, but they also remove little water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give it room to breathe. Manuals typically ask for several inches to a foot of clearance around the intake and exhaust, so do not push it against the bed, behind curtains or into a closet with the door shut. A hard, level floor or a sturdy low stand is better than thick carpet, which can block bottom intakes and transmit vibration. Keep it away from the wall behind the headboard, because vibration carries through walls and bed frames.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close the bedroom door while it runs. A dehumidifier in a bedroom with the door open is trying to dry the hallway and the rest of the house as well. With the door closed, a 22-pint unit can bring a typical bedroom down to 50 percent and then cycle off, which is quieter and cheaper than running continuously against the whole home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overnight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Getting Through the Night
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bedrooms rarely have a floor drain, so continuous drainage is usually off the table and the bucket decides how long the unit runs. Retailers list a 7-pint bucket for the GE ADYR22LC, and Housefresh reports a 7-pint tank on Honeywell's TP30WKN sibling. On a humid summer night, a 22-pint unit running hard could fill a 7-pint bucket in well under a day, but in a closed bedroom that is already near target it will run far less and the bucket will usually last.
          </p>

          <p>
            Empty the bucket before bed. It is the single habit that prevents both the 3 a.m. full-bucket beep and the unit shutting off halfway through the night. If you find the bucket is full every morning, that is a sign the room or the house has a bigger moisture problem than a bedroom unit should be solving alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the bedroom is next to a bathroom or laundry with a drain, or on a slab with a nearby sink, a drain hose may be possible. Units with a built-in pump can push water up to a sink or out a window, but they are louder and larger, and most bedroom buyers are better off with a bucket. If you do need a pump, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">best dehumidifier with pump</a> guide covers the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thermoelectric units have small tanks too. Pro Breeze lists a 1.5-liter tank on the PB-03, about 3 pints, with automatic shut-off when it fills. Because these units remove water so slowly, the tank usually lasts a day or more, and they shut off silently when full. That convenience is one reason people buy them for guest rooms and nurseries, as long as the moisture problem is small.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Running Cost and Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 22-pint compressor dehumidifier draws a few hundred watts while the compressor runs and much less when it is only circulating air. In a closed bedroom it will cycle, so its real draw depends on how damp the room is. Our <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">dehumidifier electricity guide</a> shows how to estimate cost from the label wattage and your electricity rate. ENERGY STAR-certified models remove more water per kilowatt-hour, which matters if the unit runs most days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your air conditioner is already a dehumidifier. In summer, a central or window AC removes moisture as it cools, and running it with the fan on auto rather than always on lets the coil drain properly. If your bedroom is only damp in summer and you have AC, check its settings and filter before buying a separate unit. A dehumidifier is most useful in spring and fall, when it is humid but not hot enough to run the AC, and in basement bedrooms that stay cool and damp.
          </p>

          <p>
            An air purifier is not a substitute. It filters particles such as dust and mold spores but does not change humidity at all; our <a href="/air-purifier-vs-dehumidifier/">air purifier vs dehumidifier</a> guide explains when you might want one, the other or both. And in winter, many bedrooms swing too dry rather than too damp, which is when a humidifier from our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">best humidifier for bedrooms</a> list makes more sense than a dehumidifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            If more than one or two rooms are damp, a single bedroom unit is the wrong tool. A larger dehumidifier in the basement or a whole-house system tied into the HVAC can dry the entire home more quietly, because the noisy part lives far from where you sleep. For one damp bedroom, though, a 22-pint unit with a published low-speed rating, placed well and run on a schedule, is the simplest fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Word Quiet on the Box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nearly every listing says quiet. Look for a published decibel figure at the lowest fan speed, and note which speed it describes. GE lists 43 dBA on low for the ADYR22LC; many makers publish nothing. A 10 dB difference sounds roughly twice as loud, so a few numbers on a spec sheet matter more than marketing adjectives.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Placing It Next to the Bed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A unit three feet from your pillow on a hard floor will sound louder than its rating and blow warm air at you. Put it across the room, on a level floor or low stand, with its exhaust pointed away from the bed and a foot of clearance. Keep it off the headboard wall, where vibration carries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It Flat Out All Night</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dehumidifiers add heat and cycle noisily when working hard. Run it in the afternoon and evening with the door closed to bring the room to about 50 percent, then let it idle or switch to sleep mode or low at bedtime. A schedule or timer does this automatically and keeps the night quieter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Basement-Size Unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 35 or 50 pint unit under the 2019 DOE test is built for basements. It is bigger, heavier and usually louder at its lowest speed than a bedroom needs. A 20 to 22 pint unit, the old 30-pint class, handles almost any bedroom and spends more of the night idle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drying a Room That Is Not Damp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If a hygrometer reads 45 to 55 percent, you do not need a dehumidifier, and running one can push the room below 30 percent, which dries skin and sinuses. Measure for a week first. The EPA range is 30 to 50 percent, and below 60 percent is the main goal for mold and mites.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

