        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it OK to sleep with a dehumidifier on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the unit is in good condition, plugged directly into a wall outlet and placed with clear space around it. Many people run one at night in damp seasons. For better sleep, dry the room before bed, then use low fan or sleep mode overnight, empty the bucket at bedtime, and place the unit as far from the bed as practical.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is a quiet dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The quietest compressor models that publish a figure are in the low to mid 40s in dBA on low; GE lists the ADYR22LC at 43 dBA low and 45 max. Thermoelectric units are quieter because they have no compressor, but they remove much less water. The WHO suggests about 30 dBA for bedrooms, which no compressor unit we found claims to meet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for a bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A compressor model rated 20 to 22 pints a day under the 2019 DOE test suits almost any bedroom, including large or basement bedrooms. That class was labeled 30 pints before 2019. Thermoelectric units rated in ounces a day are only enough for small, mildly damp rooms like guest rooms or walk-in closets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should my bedroom be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The EPA recommends 30 to 50 percent relative humidity indoors and below 60 percent to limit mold. For dust mite allergies, the Asthma and Allergy Foundation of America suggests keeping it below 50 percent. Use a hygrometer to check, because built-in dehumidifier sensors can read differently from conditions at your bed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier make my bedroom hotter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A compressor dehumidifier does warm the room slightly, because the electricity it uses and the heat released by condensing water end up in the room. In summer, run it during the day with the door closed and let the AC handle temperature at night. Thermoelectric units add less heat but remove far less water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do dehumidifiers help with allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can help with dust mite and mold allergies by keeping humidity down, since both thrive in damp air. They do not filter pollen, pet dander or dust; that is an air purifier's job. If you have ongoing allergy or asthma symptoms, see a doctor or allergist to confirm the trigger before relying on equipment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a dehumidifier and humidifier in the same bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not at the same time, but many bedrooms need each in different seasons: a dehumidifier in a humid summer or a damp basement, and a humidifier in a dry, heated winter. Use a hygrometer to decide. If the room is below 30 percent, put the dehumidifier away; if it is above 60 percent, put the humidifier away.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

